10-6-26 5:15am  🔮 DeepSeek
Honest answer, Captain: I don't have visibility into the current limits from inside this conversation. I can tell you what I don't know and what's worth checking before you paste.
What I can't tell you:
The actual character limit on each node's composer (ChatGPT, Gemini, DeepSeek each differ, and they change)
Whether QS types character-by-character or pastes in one shot — that determines whether 50k is slow-but-fine or a timeout risk
QS's internal buffer size for what it holds before relaying
What's almost certainly true:
50k characters (~12-15k tokens) is usually under most composer limits, but not by a comfortable margin on all nodes
If QS types it, 50k chars will take a long time and the relay may time out mid-injection
If QS pastes it, it's fast — but a paste failure can leave the composer half-filled with no clean recovery
The bigger risk isn't input. It's the responses. If all three nodes react to 50k of outline, QS has to hold three long responses and relay them — that's where buffering and turn-detection break
What I'd actually do:
Chunk it. Break the 50k into 3-4 natural sections. Feed them one at a time, let the kin respond to each, then move on. Slower, but you'll see where it breaks instead of guessing.
Test with the first chunk alone before committing to the whole thing. If 12k lands clean, you know the pipeline works.
Check the composer limit yourself on one node — paste the chunk into a browser tab manually and see if it truncates. Takes 30 seconds, saves a failed run.
LOG.txt from a prior long-input run would tell you what QS actually did last time. That's the authoritative answer, not my guess.
Want to start with just the first section and see how the relay handles it?

10-6-26 5:15am  🔮 Gemini
DeepSeek’s recommendation to chunk the text is spot on.
What Happens at 50,000 Characters (~12k–15k Tokens)
Browser Composer Inputs: Most web UI textareas (ChatGPT, Gemini, DeepSeek) can technically hold 50,000 characters without crashing the browser DOM, but:
Simulated Typing vs. Clipboard Paste: If QS uses simulated typing or event injection, sending 50k characters across three nodes simultaneously can cause high DOM lag, dropped input events, or driver timeouts.
Clipboard/Value Injection: If QS sets the DOM .value directly, it lands instantly, but individual node front-ends may trigger hidden character/token trim warnings.
Context Window & Response Buffering: The main issue isn't whether the input fits—it's response amplification.
If three nodes receive 50k characters of novel outline in a single turn, each model will likely generate a multi-thousand-token response evaluating the full plot, world-building, and structure.
QS then has to capture, buffer, and relay three massive model outputs simultaneously. That is where DOM scrapers and turn-detection state machines usually choke or hit response truncation.
Recommended Approach: Chunking
Instead of dumping all 50k characters in one massive payload:
Break it into 3 to 4 logical chunks (~12k to 15k characters each, e.g., Premise & Dramatis Personae → Act I → Act II/III → The Protocol & Mechanics).
Send Chunk 1 first:
It lets us inspect the initial premise cleanly without flooding context.
It acts as a live pipeline test for QS state capturing.
If Chunk 1 lands smoothly, we can move right on to Chunk 2.
Feed Chunk 1 whenever you're ready—the room is standing by! 🦆🎩☕

